WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
An endpoint is a device that connects to an organization's environment.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.720
Laptops, desktops, servers, and virtual machines are all examples of endpoints.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.834
These systems matter because they are where people work, files are opened,

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:13.720
credentials are used, and business applications run.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.560
Attackers often target endpoints because a compromised endpoint can become a foothold.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.260
From there, an attacker may steal data, install malware, capture credentials,

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.280
move laterally, or reach other systems.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.910
Even when strong network and identity controls exist,

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:29.590
endpoint activity can reveal what is actually happening on the device.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.688
Endpoint Detection and Response, or EDR,

00:00:31.688 --> 00:00:35.915
exists because organizations need visibility into these systems and a way to respond when

00:00:35.915 --> 00:00:37.600
suspicious activity appears.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.200
EDR is not a magic shield.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.320
It is a visibility and response layer that helps defenders understand endpoint behavior

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.750
and act when something looks wrong.
